Based on your current integration in `Hann8n/orbyt`, here is a research analysis and development plan to fix your merging issues and implement the new editing suite.

### 1. Solving "Unclean Cuts" & Merging Different Clip Types

Your current implementation uses a **"Normalize then Concat"** strategy. You re-encode every individual clip to a common format and then use the `concat` demuxer.

**The Problem:**
The `concat` demuxer with `-c copy` is extremely fragile. Even if you normalize clips, slight differences in **Audio Sample Rate** (e.g., 44.1kHz vs 48kHz), **Timebase**, or **Pixel Aspect Ratio (SAR)** will cause glitches (stuttering audio, green frames) at the cut points. This is why mixing Camera video (often variable frame rate) and Uploaded video (diverse encodings) fails.

**The Solution: Single-Pass Complex Filter**
Instead of normalizing individual files and then merging, you should use an FFmpeg **Complex Filter**. This accepts raw inputs of any type, scales them internally, and outputs a single, perfectly smooth encoded file. This guarantees clean cuts because the video is generated as one continuous stream.

**Updated `mergeSegments` Logic:**

```typescript name=src/services/VideoEditingService.ts
import { FFmpegKit, ReturnCode } from 'ffmpeg-kit-react-native';

// ... existing interfaces

static async mergeSegmentsComplex(segments: VideoSegment[], outputPath: string): Promise<string> {
  // 1. Construct Inputs
  let inputCmd = '';
  let filterGraph = '';

  // Target resolution (e.g., 1080x1920)
  const W = 1080;
  const H = 1920;

  segments.forEach((segment, index) => {
    const path = segment.video.uri || segment.video.path; // Handle both types
    inputCmd += `-i "${path}" `;

    // 2. Build Filter Chain for each input
    // Scale to fit box, add black padding if aspect ratio differs, set SAR to 1 (square pixels)
    filterGraph += `[${index}:v]scale=${W}:${H}:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=${W}:${H}:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2,setsar=1[v${index}];`;
    // Force audio resampling to common 44.1kHz stereo
    filterGraph += `[${index}:a]aformat=sample_rates=44100:channel_layouts=stereo[a${index}];`;
  });

  // 3. Concat Filter
  segments.forEach((_, i) => {
    filterGraph += `[v${i}][a${i}]`;
  });
  filterGraph += `concat=n=${segments.length}:v=1:a=1[outv][outa]`;

  // 4. Final Command
  // preset ultrafast for preview, medium for final export
  const cmd = `${inputCmd} -filter_complex "${filterGraph}" -map "[outv]" -map "[outa]" -c:v libx264 -preset ultrafast -c:a aac "${outputPath}"`;

  const session = await FFmpegKit.execute(cmd);
  if (ReturnCode.isSuccess(await session.getReturnCode())) {
    return outputPath;
  } else {
    throw new Error('Merge failed');
  }
}
```

### 2. Building the Edit Screen Features

Here is how to implement the specific features you requested using `ffmpeg-kit`.

#### A. Rearranging Clips
Since you are already using `react-native-draggable-flatlist`, this is purely a state management task. When the user drags to reorder, simply update your `segments` array order before passing it to the `mergeSegments` function above.

#### B. Placing Text on Video
Use the `drawtext` filter. You must escape special characters in the text carefully.

```typescript
static async addTextOverlay(
  videoPath: string, 
  outputPath: string, 
  text: string, 
  options: { x: string; y: string; size: number; color: string }
) {
  // Ensure fontfile path is accessible. For Android/iOS, you often need to copy a .ttf file to cache dir first.
  const fontPath = ""; // Path to local .ttf file

  // FFmpeg requires colons and single quotes to be escaped
  const safeText = text.replace(/:/g, '\\:').replace(/'/g, "\\'");

  // x=(w-text_w)/2 centers horizontally
  const filter = `drawtext=text='${safeText}':fontfile=${fontPath}:fontsize=${options.size}:fontcolor=${options.color}:x=${options.x}:y=${options.y}`;

  const cmd = `-i "${videoPath}" -vf "${filter}" -c:a copy "${outputPath}"`;
  await FFmpegKit.execute(cmd);
}
```

#### C. Adjusting Sound & Background Music
To mix original audio with new background music (e.g., lowering original volume), use the `amix` filter.

```typescript
static async addBackgroundMusic(
  videoPath: string, 
  musicPath: string, 
  outputPath: string,
  volumes: { videoVol: number; musicVol: number } // e.g. 0.5, 1.0
) {
  // [0:a] is video audio, [1:a] is music
  // input 0 volume -> [a0]
  // input 1 volume -> [a1]
  // [a0][a1] amix -> mix them
  // duration=first -> end when video ends
  const filter = `[0:a]volume=${volumes.videoVol}[a0];[1:a]volume=${volumes.musicVol}[a1];[a0][a1]amix=inputs=2:duration=first[outa]`;

  const cmd = `-i "${videoPath}" -i "${musicPath}" -filter_complex "${filter}" -map 0:v -map "[outa]" -c:v copy -c:a aac "${outputPath}"`;

  await FFmpegKit.execute(cmd);
}
```

